This aircraft is a JSBSim model of Arrow Air's Quiver Dev-Kit quadcopter, written by PteroLabs in 2026 from Arrow
Air's open hardware design.

Quiver is Arrow Air's design: Project Quiver, https://github.com/Arrow-air/project-quiver, licensed under the CERN
Open Hardware Licence Version 2 - Strongly Reciprocal (CERN-OHL-S-2.0). This folder is a modification of that Source
and is distributed under the same licence; see LICENSE in this folder for its text. Modified by PteroLabs,
2026-10-20: the design was turned into a simulation model, and nothing here changes the hardware.

From Project Quiver, commit 9ef9efc80db1 (2026-09-29):

  meshes/: built from the assembly's STEP files (visible parts), converted to glTF.

  Positions and dimensions in Metrics.xml, Mass.xml, Gear.xml, Propulsion.xml, Sensors.xml and Visual.xml: the CAD
  source under src/quiver/ (each file's comments name the assembly it comes from).

  Masses: the Dev-Kit Engineering Report, Weight & Payload Summary.

  The propeller blade geometry behind Engines/MFP2480.xml: the prop's STEP file (3112_propeller.step).

  firmwares/ardupilot_quiver.param: Arrow's standard-params.param (commit a14970474c, 2026-06-17), with the changes
  SITL needs; each change is marked in the file.

Other sources:

  Hobbywing XRotor X6 Plus G2 spec sheet and its 14S static load table with the MFP 24x8.0 prop: motor constants,
  thrust, torque and current (Engines/, Systems/esc_map.xml).

  Tattu 4.0 30000mAh 14S1P 53.2V HV smart battery: the product page (capacity, internal resistance bound) and the
  Tattu 4.0 14S smart battery user manual (charge limit, alarm voltage, static SOC table) (Battery.xml,
  Batteries/LiHV.xml).
    https://www.tattuworld.com/products/tattu-4-0-30000mah-14s1p-53-2v-hv-35c-smart-uav-lipo-battery.html
    https://cdn.tattuworld.com/uploads/soft/20260812/1-260Q21P604505.pdf

  The datasheets of the avionics in Arrow's equipment list (RPLidar S2L, NanoRadar MR82, Ainstein US-D1, Benewake
  TF03, Holybro DroneCAN F9P, Matek M9N, SIYI A8 mini and HM30, Raspberry Pi 5, BotBlox GigaBlox Nano, BlueMark
  db201) for the avionics power in Battery.xml.

  Barai et al. (2018) for the ratio of DC to AC resistance in pouch cells, cited in Battery.xml.

Chosen, with no source, and marked so where they appear: the body's drag coefficients, the motor bell's inertia, the
blade polar of the prop's BEMT tables, the mass not covered by the CAD parts, the pads' friction, the gimbal's rate
and lag, the ESC map below its first table row.

